    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Although safety standards have reduced fatal head trauma due to single severe\r\nhead impacts, mild trauma from repeated head exposures may carry risks of\r\nlong-term chronic changes in the brain’s function and structure. To study the\r\nphysical sensitivities of the brain to mild head impacts, we developed the first\r\ndynamic model of the skull–brain based on in vivo MRI data. We showed that\r\nthe motion of the brain can be described by a rigid-body with constrained kinematics.\r\nWe further demonstrated that skull–brain dynamics can be\r\napproximated by an under-damped system with a low-frequency resonance at\r\naround 15 Hz. Furthermore, from our previous field measurements, we found\r\nthat head motions in a variety of activities, including contact sports, show a primary\r\nfrequency of less than 20 Hz. This implies that typical head exposures may\r\ndrive the brain dangerously close to its mechanical resonance and lead to amplified\r\nbrain–skull relative motions. Our results suggest a possible cause for mild\r\nbrain trauma, which could occur due to repetitive low-acceleration head\r\noscillations in a variety of recreational and occupational activities.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Laksari, Kaveh, et al. \"Resonance of human brain under head acceleration.\" Journal of The Royal Society Interface 12.108 (2015): 20150331.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
